```js
async function getUser(id) {
  const rows = await db.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?', [id]);
  if (!rows.length) throw new Error('Not found');
  return rows[0];
}
```

Call it with `try/catch` or `.catch()`:

```js
// try/catch
try {
  const user = await getUser(id);
} catch (err) {
  // handles both query errors and 'Not found'
}

// or promise chain
getUser(id).then(user => ...).catch(err => ...);
```

This assumes `db.query` returns a promise — most modern drivers (pg, mysql2, better-sqlite3) do. If yours doesn't, wrap it first with `util.promisify(db.query.bind(db))`.
